# Telling buyers about a new version

The app can tell its user that a newer version exists. It does this by reading one small
file from an address that is fixed when you build the app. There is no server of yours, no
account, and nothing is installed automatically: the user sees a notice with your notes and
your download instructions, and gets the new version the same way they got the first one.

## What the app does

- When it starts, and once a day while it runs, it reads the file at the address in
  `update-address.json`.
- If the file names a version later than the one running, the dashboard shows a bell notice
  and a line on the Live tab with your notes and download instructions.
- The request carries nothing about the user: no version, no id, no settings, no cookie.
  Whoever hosts the file sees what any web host sees: that some internet address read it.
- Offline, or the file is missing or broken: nothing happens and nothing is shown.
- The user can switch the check off (Connect tab, "About this app").
- With an empty address (`"url": ""`) the app never asks. That is how the project ships
  until you set an address.

## The file

```json
{
  "version": "1.1.0",
  "date": "2027-01-15",
  "notes": "Adds two host voices and fixes the gift board on small screens.",
  "downloadInfo": "Download the new version from your order page, unzip it into a new folder, and use Connect > Bring my settings over."
}
```

- `version`: numbers and dots only. It must be later than the `version` in `package.json`
  of the copies you want to notify.
- `date`, `notes`, `downloadInfo`: plain text, up to 1000 characters each. They are shown as
  text, so a web address in them is not clickable; the user copies it.
- The whole file must stay under 20,000 characters. `docs/update-example.json` is a copy to
  start from.

## Releasing a new version

1. Change `version` in `package.json` (for example `1.0.0` to `1.1.0`).
2. Run `npm run portable`, zip `artifacts/portable/XeTuiLIVE`, and upload the zip where your
   buyers download it.
3. Only then edit the published file so it names the new version. Buyers who start the app
   after that see the notice within a day.

## Setting the address (once)

Put the address in `update-address.json` before you build:

```json
{ "url": "https://…/update.json" }
```

It must start with `https://`. Every copy built after that asks this address, so choose one
you can keep for years: a copy already sold cannot be told about a new address.

## Where to publish the file for free

Two ways, both free. The difference is who can find the file.

### A public page (GitHub Pages)

Anyone who knows or guesses the address can read the file, and the page shows up under your
GitHub account name. Since the file only says "version 1.1.0 exists, download it from your
order page", there is usually nothing in it to hide.

1. Create a free account at github.com (the account name becomes part of the address).
2. Create a new **public** repository, for example `xetui-updates`.
3. Add a file named `update.json` with the content above ("Add file" > "Create new file").
4. In the repository: Settings > Pages > "Deploy from a branch" > branch `main`, folder
   `/ (root)` > Save.
5. After a minute the address is `https://<account>.github.io/xetui-updates/update.json`.
   Open it in a browser to check, then put it in `update-address.json`.
6. To announce a version later: open the file on github.com, press the pencil, change it,
   and press "Commit changes".

### A private link (a secret GitHub Gist)

The file has a long random address that is not listed anywhere. Only someone who has the
address can read it, but the address is inside every copy of the app, so any buyer can find
it. "Private" here means "not advertised", not "protected".

1. Sign in at gist.github.com.
2. Name the file `update.json`, paste the content, and choose **Create secret gist**.
3. Press **Raw**. Remove the long commit code from that address so it reads
   `https://gist.githubusercontent.com/<account>/<gist id>/raw/update.json`: this form always
   gives the latest version of the file. Open it in a browser to check.
4. Put that address in `update-address.json`.
5. To announce a version later: open the gist, press Edit, change it, and press "Update".
   The raw address can take a few minutes to show the change.

Either way, do not put anything in the file that you would not show a stranger: no licence
keys, no buyer names, no direct download link you want to keep for paying customers.
